WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.634
Tax season often starts with a folder of PDFs and a quiet worry.

00:00:07.634 --> 00:00:10.312
Which page did that number come from?

00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:11.615
Is a form missing?

00:00:11.615 --> 00:00:15.525
And do you really want to hand all of it to a chatbot?

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.585
Every number, traced to its page.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.889
This is taxtrace, running in your browser, on your own Mac.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:26.911
At the top, your to-do list, and the year's tax-saving moves,

00:00:26.911 --> 00:00:29.350
ranked by the dollars each one saves.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.904
Further down are your documents, in a plain folder on your disk,

00:00:35.904 --> 00:00:37.099
sorted by tax year.

00:00:37.099 --> 00:00:39.740
Each one is tagged with the form it holds,

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.255
and none of them has left your computer.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.397
Behind the page, taxtrace reads each form box by box.

00:00:50.397 --> 00:00:56.090
Here is the W-2 from Initech: eight values read, and all five checks pass,

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:59.552
including that box 4 is 6.2 percent of box 3.

00:00:59.552 --> 00:01:04.245
Every value keeps the page, and the exact text, it came from.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.744
From those readings, taxtrace writes an entry sheet:

00:01:09.744 --> 00:01:13.556
everything your tax software will ask, in the order it asks.

00:01:13.556 --> 00:01:16.288
Each row is ready, an estimate, or missing,

00:01:16.288 --> 00:01:19.465
and a missing row names the command that fills it.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.359
Last, the chat, for questions about tax rules and forms.

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:27.964
Before anything goes to a cloud model,

00:01:27.964 --> 00:01:32.009
digits become pound signs and names and towns become stars,

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:34.820
and the page shows exactly what was sent.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.621
And a question with an amount in it never leaves your Mac.

00:01:39.621 --> 00:01:42.815
The model here answers it, and the page says why.
